Chapter: MicInput
  cites Kernel chapter HdaAudio
  grounds Device.Mmio

 Both take a reading of the microphone's loudness -- the peak absolute
 amplitude over a short capture window. A quiet room reads under ~15; a
 voice or a clap reads in the hundreds to thousands. is-quiet compares
 that to a threshold; listen returns it as text. Each call opens the mic
 (the first capture), settles a few ticks so codex-vm's capture ring
 fills, then reads a real window -- so a reading costs ~200 ms, which is
 the cost of not trusting a cold ring.

 We say:

Section: The Microphone Effect

  mic-quiet-threshold : Integer = 40
  mic-window-bytes : Integer = 16384
  mic-ring-bytes : Integer = 262144

  mic-settle : Integer -> [Microphone] Integer
  mic-settle (target) =
    if get-ticks >= target then 0
    else mic-settle target

  mic-peak : Integer -> [Microphone] Integer
  mic-peak (nbytes) = act
    warm <- hda-capture 4096
    s <- mic-settle (get-ticks + 4)
    buf <- hda-capture nbytes
    let p = hda-peak buf (nbytes / 4)
    in p
  end

  mic-ms-bytes : Integer -> Integer
  mic-ms-bytes (ms) =
    let raw = hda-sample-rate * ms / 1000 * 4
    in if raw > mic-ring-bytes then mic-ring-bytes
    else if raw < 4096 then 4096
    else raw

  is-quiet : [Microphone] Boolean
  is-quiet = mic-peak mic-window-bytes < mic-quiet-threshold

  listen : Integer -> [Microphone] Text
  listen (ms) = "mic-level:" & show (mic-peak (mic-ms-bytes ms))
